      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "header",
          "bbox": [
            0.648,
            0.047,
            0.846,
            0.064
          ],
          "content": "Working with Fractions"
        },
        {
          "type": "equation",
          "bbox": [
            0.402,
            0.086,
            0.596,
            0.15
          ],
          "content": "\\frac { \\frac { 1 } { 2 } \\times 5 } { 7 \\times 2 4 } = \\frac { 1 \\times 5 } { 7 \\times 2 } = \\frac { 5 } { 1 4 }",
          "image_source": "images/page_10_equation_1.jpg"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.174,
            0.157,
            0.781,
            0.178
          ],
          "content": "Let us use the same technique to do one more multiplication."
        },
        {
          "type": "equation",
          "bbox": [
            0.459,
            0.188,
            0.54,
            0.226
          ],
          "content": "\\frac { 1 4 } { 1 5 } \\times \\frac { 2 5 } { 4 2 }",
          "image_source": "images/page_10_equation_3.jpg"
        },
        {
          "type": "equation",
          "bbox": [
            0.404,
            0.233,
            0.591,
            0.302
          ],
          "content": "\\frac{1}{3} \\times \\frac{5}{3} = \\frac{1 \\times 5}{3 \\times 3} = \\frac{5}{9}",
          "image_source": "images/page_10_equation_4.jpg"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.149,
            0.308,
            0.849,
            0.384
          ],
          "content": "When multiplying fractions, we can first divide the numerator and denominator by their common factors before multiplying the numerators and denominators. This is called cancelling the common factors."
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph_title",
          "bbox": [
            0.151,
            0.401,
            0.381,
            0.424
          ],
          "level": 2,
          "content": "A Pinch of History"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.149,
            0.432,
            0.848,
            0.509
          ],
          "content": "In India, the process of reducing a fraction to its lowest terms — known as apavartana — is so well known that it finds mention even in a non-mathematical work. A Jaina scholar Umasvati (c. 150 CE) used it as a simile in a philosophical work."
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph_title",
          "bbox": [
            0.111,
            0.515,
            0.293,
            0.54
          ],
          "level": 2,
          "content": "Figure it Out"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.151,
            0.548,
            0.849,
            0.617
          ],
          "content": "1. A water tank is filled from a tap. If the tap is open for 1 hour, $\\frac { 7 } { 1 0 }$ of the tank gets filled. How much of the tank is filled if the tap is open for"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.207,
            0.624,
            0.449,
            0.661
          ],
          "content": "(a) $\\frac { 1 } { 3 }$ hour"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.207,
            0.665,
            0.444,
            0.7
          ],
          "content": "$\\frac{2}{3}$ hour \\_\\_\\_\\_\\_\\_\\_\\_"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.207,
            0.705,
            0.446,
            0.739
          ],
          "content": "(c) 3 hour \\_\\_\\_\\_\\_ $\\frac { 3 } { 4 }$"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.208,
            0.744,
            0.456,
            0.779
          ],
          "content": "(d) $\\frac { 7 } { 1 0 }$"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.206,
            0.779,
            0.82,
            0.802
          ],
          "content": "(e) For the tank to be full, how long should the tap be running?"
        },
        {
          "type": "image",
          "bbox": [
            0.829,
            0.76,
            0.929,
            0.816
          ],
          "content": "",
          "captions": [],
          "footnotes": [],
          "image_source": "images/page_10_image_15.jpg"
        },
        {
